{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat draft_reference_gaps.md && printf '\\\\n--- GAP JSON ---\\\\n' && cat draft_reference_gaps.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Draft Reference Gaps\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nTwo paragraph-specific gaps justify a small retrieval pass for Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark context and direct Rasch/IRT foundations. Existing related-work coverage needs no expansion. Both targets are medium priority; neither requires changing the paper's evidence-bounded thesis.\n\n## Grounded Review\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, all 25 entries in `references.bib` and their existing provenance, the 30 claims in `claims_grounding.json`, the structure and final-evaluation evidence in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. All manuscript citation keys resolve, and every existing bibliography entry is cited. Global memory was used only as qualitative context, not numerical evidence. No references were retrieved.\n\n## 1. Benchmark Collection Context\n\n- **Target ID:** `evaluation-leaderboard-context`\n- **Section:** Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability\n- **Paragraph:** Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts, opening paragraph.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / benchmark_context\n- **Anchor:** “four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)”\n- **Gap:** The paragraph identifies a named upstream collection without a direct collection reference; no dedicated Leaderboard source appears in the bibliography. HELM is already represented by `Liang2023`, which can be reused here without new retrieval. `Wang2024` concerns MMLU-Pro and must not be repurposed as provenance for the staged MMLU case.\n- **Focused search topic:** Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation covering BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Add one authoritative collection citation. Retain the staged names and the explicit warning against assuming public-benchmark identity. Case counts, fixed item universes, snapshot identity, and filtering remain supported by the supplied artifacts, not by this citation. Do not commission separate searches for all ten benchmarks.\n\n## 2. Named Item-Response Model Foundations\n\n- **Target ID:** `method-rasch-irt-foundations`\n- **Section:** Method\n- **Paragraph:** Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction, introduction of Rasch smoothing.\n- **Priority / intent:** medium / background\n- **Anchor:** “The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.”\n- **Gap:** The named response family lacks a direct foundational citation. `Andersen1970` is cited in Related Work for an estimation procedure explicitly not used here, and its stored abstract addresses conditional estimation. Broad statistical texts and the already cited LLM compression methods do not directly anchor the Rasch/2PL distinction in these paragraphs. The local IRT comparator in Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability also introduces two-parameter logistic responses and fitted item information without a direct foundation citation.\n- **Focused search topics:** Rasch logistic ability–difficulty model foundations; two-parameter logistic discrimination and Fisher item information.\n- **Bounded intervention:** Retrieve one authoritative source covering both families, or at most two complementary sources. Cite the Rasch introduction and reuse the relevant source at the local IRT comparator if supported. Preserve the fixed-ability, penalized custom-fit description and the disclaimer that local references are not official reproductions. No claim of profile-component benefit or target-calibrated uncertainty should be added.\n\n## Coverage That Does Not Justify Retrieval\n\n- Related Work directly cites Anchor Points, tinyBenchmarks, metabench, reference-instance prediction, and active evaluation acquisition. There is no missing comparator-family paragraph to expand.\n- Scalar-target design, ridge regression, validation dependence, domain shift, missingness, ranking statistics, and bootstrap background already have relevant citations. The selector is explicitly a heuristic, not an unsupported optimality theorem.\n- Numerical accuracy, availability masks, failed qualification, structural diagnostics, and local implementation details are grounded in the supplied evidence. More citations cannot repair missing predictions, isolate a profile contribution, or establish overall superiority.\n- Do not add literature on deployment speedups, new benchmark populations, target-adaptive policies, or calibrated stopping rules: those would expand rather than support the existing story.\n\n## Handoff\n\nRetrieve approximately two to three sources total across these two targets, preferring reuse when one IRT source covers both model families. This memo does not prescribe new paper sections or a broad rewrite. The manuscript, bibliography, and reference provenance remain unchanged.\n\n--- GAP JSON ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"Two paragraph-specific gaps justify a small retrieval pass for Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark context and direct Rasch/IRT foundations; the existing related-work coverage needs no expansion.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection provenance\",\n    \"Rasch and two-parameter logistic item-response model foundations\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"evaluation-leaderboard-context\",\n      \"section\": \"Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"The opening benchmark paragraph names four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases without a direct reference to that collection, and the bibliography contains no dedicated Leaderboard source. Liang2023 already supplies HELM context and can be reused locally without retrieval. One authoritative Leaderboard collection reference would identify the upstream evaluation context; it must not be used to substantiate the staged case counts, exact snapshot, filtering, or benchmark-version equivalence, which remain artifact-grounded.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math)\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Open LLM Leaderboard benchmark collection documentation BBH GPQA MuSR OpenLLM Math\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-rasch-irt-foundations\",\n      \"section\": \"Method\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"background\",\n      \"reason\": \"The reconstruction paragraph introduces a Rasch response model without a direct foundational citation. Related Work cites Andersen1970 specifically to distinguish conditional maximum-likelihood estimation, not to explain the response-model family; its stored abstract concerns conditional estimation. The later local comparator uses a two-parameter logistic model and fitted item information, also without a direct foundational reference. One or two authoritative IRT sources could support these named model families and their distinction without implying that the custom fitting procedure is standard, that the local comparator reproduces a published system, or that the profile branch has an established empirical benefit.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model.\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Rasch logistic item-response model ability item difficulty foundational reference\",\n        \"two-parameter logistic item-response model discrimination Fisher item information authoritative reference\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*reference_query_plan*' -g 'SKILL.md' -g 'memory.md'; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- GLOBAL MEMORY ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory_summary.md; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; cat paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*reference_query_plan*' -g 'SKILL.md' -g 'memory.md'; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- GLOBAL MEMORY ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory_summary.md; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; cat paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace/paper\nreference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261113_180124.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261113_180124.log\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\nmemory.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.json\n\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Predicting an LLM's full binary benchmark score and model ranking from 8, 16, or 32 source-selected items using group-aware score-covariance design and a ridge/Rasch-residual profile blend, with limited generalization evidence on held-out benchmark cases.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Fixed nested item selection by squared conditional covariance with the source full score divided by conditional item variance, using a low-rank pivoted factorization\",\n    \"Equal source-group weighting for acquisition, Rasch fitting, particle priors, and grouped tuning losses\",\n    \"Ridge full-score regression with shrinkage toward the training-source mean\",\n    \"Rasch-shrunk source response profiles with discrete ability-offset particles and Bernoulli posterior updating\",\n    \"Exact observed-score contribution plus posterior expected unobserved-score contribution\",\n    \"Source-only group-disjoint tuning of a convex linear/profile-posterior blend\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Response-vector clustering anchors and weighted score estimation: Anchor Points\",\n    \"IRT-assisted benchmark compression and score estimation: tinyBenchmarks and metabench\",\n    \"Adaptive evaluation acquisition, as a related selection family rather than an implemented target-adaptive policy\",\n    \"Reference-instance prediction of unseen model performance: 100 instances is all you need\",\n    \"Score-targeted experimental design versus representation-diversity selection\",\n    \"Uniform item sampling and raw sample means; uniform selection with the learned predictor; source-mean, constant, copy, and IRT controls\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Development/search: ARC-Challenge, IFEval, MMLU-Pro, from staged Open LLM Leaderboard matrices\",\n    \"Final: bbh, gpqa, musr, openllm_math, from staged Open LLM Leaderboard matrices\",\n    \"Final: commonsense, gsm, legalbench, math, med_qa, mmlu, from staged HELM matrices\",\n    \"Pinned benchmark-prediction score-matrix snapshot: 55faff496947789f94e6b5908c77cbc3e84f81ad; evaluation uses staged, filtered fixed item universes\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Limited generalization from development benchmarks to held-out benchmark cases: the selected method's final retained MAE is worse than its search MAE, and final evaluation did not qualify\",\n    \"Selection and estimation must be separated: uniform selection with the learned predictor has lower retained final MAE than score-targeted selection, whereas the ordering reverses in search\",\n    \"Raw subset averaging and learned full-score prediction are distinct: the no_calibration mode returns the observed mean rather than merely removing an affine calibration layer\",\n    \"Model-family dependence motivates group-disjoint source tuning, but opaque groups and heuristic family metadata do not certify true ancestry or architecture transfer\",\n    \"Ranking correlations and retained MAE are descriptive evidence, not proof of calibrated uncertainty, statistical superiority, or a diagnosed cause of final non-qualification\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference for response-vector clustering and weighted anchor estimates; directly contextualizes the recorded anchor comparator without equating that comparator to an audited official reproduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference combining anchors and IRT; essential context for the recorded IRT comparator and the implemented Rasch-based score predictor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"metabench A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in LLMs\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference for IRT-informed item selection and learned full-score prediction; contrasts information-based selection with the implemented full-score covariance objective.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Active Evaluation Acquisition for Efficient LLM Benchmarking\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference for learned adaptive acquisition; establishes the boundary between target-adaptive selection and this run's fixed source-only nested prefixes, not an unrun head-to-head comparison.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"c-optimal experimental design prediction linear functional\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The selector targets conditional uncertainty in a scalar full score rather than general representation diversity. Classical linear-functional design is the relevant methodological neighborhood; exact c-optimality of this implementation is not claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"latent class Rasch models local dependence item responses\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The selected configuration mixes Rasch-shrunk empirical source profiles and discrete ability offsets. Mixture psychometrics and local-dependence literature contextualize that representation and its conditional-independence limitations without claiming equivalence to a particular published model.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"100 instances is all you need: predicting the success of a new LLM on unseen data by testing on a few instances\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference for predicting unobserved instance outcomes from reference-instance responses; directly relevant to source-profile inference and the observed/unobserved score decomposition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"grouped cross-validation hierarchical dependent data prediction assessment\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Group-disjoint source tuning is implemented, while the search/final selection-control reversal motivates caution about generalization. Classical dependent-data validation is relevant; the reversal alone does not establish family shift or a causal explanation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models HELM\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference for the source evaluation framework underlying six final benchmark matrices; cite dataset context without treating framework-wide claims or the raw upstream release as properties of the staged fixed item universes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard v2 Fourrier Habib Lozovskaya Szafer Wolf\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Permitted starter reference for the leaderboard context underlying the development matrices and four final matrices; future retrieval must verify benchmark-specific provenance rather than assume every staged case is an official v2 component.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify, infer, search for, or access the task-prohibited unnamed paper or its authors, title, findings, mirrors, summaries, reviews, presentations, repositories, or discussions\",\n    \"Citation-graph expansion, citing/cited-by traversal, related-paper recommendations, or unvetted review/aggregation pages that could expose material citing or discussing the prohibited work\",\n    \"Broad topic-wide LLM benchmark-prediction searches outside the permitted exact-title seeds; the three methodological queries are restricted to classical experimental-design, psychometric, and dependent-data validation sources\",\n    \"Generic optimization, general benchmarking, scientific method, paper writing, campaign chronology, milestones, or search-history queries\",\n    \"Claims of Transformer-to-Mamba, dense-to-MoE, architecture transfer, or ancestry-verified generalization without an evaluated architecture intervention\",\n    \"Claims that fixed prefixes implement target-adaptive testing, or that the residual-profile mixture is an exact reproduction of a named comparator\",\n    \"Claims of universal superiority, calibrated posterior uncertainty, statistically significant gains, certified lineage independence, or a known cause of final non-qualification\",\n    \"Continuous-score evaluation, open-ended sample pooling, multimodal evaluation, or neural-network training queries not exercised by the binary fixed-benchmark run\",\n    \"Treating an upstream benchmark name, file key, leaderboard component, and audited staged item universe as interchangeable\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- GLOBAL MEMORY ---\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-13 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Wrote Lineage-Held-Out Anchor-Audit Residual Estimation. gate=updated; trigger=b; reason=Supplied identity and explicit ancestry metadata enable an auditable grouped stress-test category absent from the empty conclusions.\n  - evidence: Mounted README documents repeated identities/details repositories, explicit Base Model metadata, verified columns, and no provided groups or scores. Bootstrap manifest reports correct=true and combined_score=0.9414127869558047; named candidate source path was unavailable. No new experiment or score. Considered richer IRT-only prediction, pure random sampling, and protocol-only work; selected a bounded source-prediction plus uniform-residual-audit intervention with conditional unbiasedness only at the raw full-correction endpoint.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_t