{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- MANUSCRIPT ANCHORS ---\\\\n'; rg -n 'section\\\\{|subsection\\\\{|nonlinear branch|two-parameter|IRT reference models|final assessment contains|Andersen1970' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- RASCH PARAGRAPH ---\\\\n'; sed -n '75,92p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- BENCHMARK PARAGRAPH ---\\\\n'; sed -n '115,121p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE TITLES ---\\\\n'; jq '.references | if type==\\\"array\\\" then .[] else .[] end | {citation_key,title,consulted_scope}' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- ANDERSEN PROVENANCE ---\\\\n'; jq '.references | .[] | select((.citation_key // .key // \\\"\\\") == \\\"Andersen1970\\\" or (.title // \\\"\\\" | startswith(\\\"Asymptotic\\\")))' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY FOOTER ---\\\\n'; tail -18 memory.md; printf '\\\\n--- CONCLUSIONS LOCATION ---\\\\n'; ls global_memory; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY HELPER OPTIONS ---\\\\n'; rg -n 'add_argument|memory.md' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- MANUSCRIPT ANCHORS ---\\\\n'; rg -n 'section\\\\{|subsection\\\\{|nonlinear branch|two-parameter|IRT reference models|final assessment contains|Andersen1970' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- RASCH PARAGRAPH ---\\\\n'; sed -n '75,92p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- BENCHMARK PARAGRAPH ---\\\\n'; sed -n '115,121p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE TITLES ---\\\\n'; jq '.references | if type==\\\"array\\\" then .[] else .[] end | {citation_key,title,consulted_scope}' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- ANDERSEN PROVENANCE ---\\\\n'; jq '.references | .[] | select((.citation_key // .key // \\\"\\\") == \\\"Andersen1970\\\" or (.title // \\\"\\\" | startswith(\\\"Asymptotic\\\")))' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY FOOTER ---\\\\n'; tail -18 memory.md; printf '\\\\n--- CONCLUSIONS LOCATION ---\\\\n'; ls global_memory; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY HELPER OPTIONS ---\\\\n'; rg -n 'add_argument|memory.md' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- MANUSCRIPT ANCHORS ---\n21:\\section{Introduction}\n41:\\section{Related Work}\n46:Choosing observations for a scalar target relates to optimal allocation in regression \\citep{Elfving1952}, whereas information criteria for a whole parameter vector address a different objective \\citep{Kiefer1960}. Our empirical covariance rule is a regularized linear-prediction heuristic, not an optimal-design theorem for shifted binary responses. Ridge regression supplies a stabilized linear estimator \\citep{Hoerl1970}; standard statistical-learning and probabilistic-modeling texts provide broader background \\citep{Hastie2009,Bishop2006}. The profile model is a finite mixture, but it does not fit mixture weights by expectation maximization \\citep{Dempster1977}. Likewise, its fixed-ability difficulty fitting should not be confused with conditional maximum-likelihood estimation \\citep{Andersen1970}.\n51:\\section{Method}\n52:\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\n59:\\subsection{Score-Targeted Fixed Prefixes}\n76:\\subsection{Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction}\n86:The nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model. Source abilities are fixed at $\\theta_i=\\operatorname{logit}[(\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}=\\sigmoid(\\theta_i-d_j)$ and\n107:\\subsection{What a Fixed Prefix Can Identify}\n113:\\section{Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability}\n114:\\subsection{Fixed Benchmarks and Grouped Model Holdouts}\n115:The final assessment contains four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math) and six HELM-derived cases (Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU). We retain these staged case names rather than asserting identity with every similarly named public benchmark. Each case uses a fixed binary item universe. The question is prediction for held-out models on that universe, not generalization to newly sampled questions or unseen benchmark distributions.\n124:\\subsection{Comparators and Control Semantics}\n130:The anchor reference clusters centered, normalized item response vectors and estimates scores from representatives weighted by assigned cluster sizes. The IRT reference fits a penalized two-parameter logistic response model, chooses items by fitted information near zero ability, and infers target ability from queried responses. These references use the same query budgets, but they are local comparators, not official reproductions of Anchor Points or tinyBenchmarks. A source-mean reference predicts the average source score without target responses. Extended fitting details are in the supplement.\n133:\\subsection{Three Distinct Reporting Objects}\n141:\\section{Results}\n142:\\subsection{Completed Evaluation, Failed Qualification}\n191:\\subsection{Conditional Comparison Across Fixed Cases}\n205:\\subsection{Item Budget, Reconstruction, and Ranking}\n221:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n234:\\section{Conclusion}\n248:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n249:\\subsection{Inspectable Implementation and Reproducibility}\n280:\\subsection{Extended Estimation Details}\n289:\\subsection{Local Reference Implementations}\n293:The IRT reference models logits as $\\theta_i\\exp(\\eta_j)-d_j$. Its objective is summed Bernoulli negative log likelihood plus penalties $0.5\\|\\theta\\|^2+0.05\\|\\eta\\|^2+0.005\\|d\\|^2$. L-BFGS-B uses bounded parameters and at most 40 iterations. Item order is the decreasing fitted information $a_j^2 p_j(1-p_j)$ at zero ability, where $a_j=\\exp(\\eta_j)$. Target abilities use up to 30 regularized Newton updates on queried responses; the reference score averages fitted response probabilities over the entire item universe. Finite-objective checks do not prove solver optimality. No exact reproduction of a published IRT benchmark-compression package is claimed.\n296:\\subsection{Available-Pair Numerical Results}\n353:\\subsection{Seed-Level Records and Exhaustive Numerical Ledger}\n413:\\subsection{Availability Masks and Statistical Interpretation}\n\n--- RASCH PARAGRAPH ---\n\n\\subsection{Linear and Residual-Profile Reconstruction}\nThe linear branch regresses source full scores on the selected binary responses. Centering features at their unweighted source mean $\\bar x_Q$ and scores at $\\bar t_{\\rm row}$ gives\n\\begin{equation}\n \\beta_b=(X_{Q,c}^{\\top}X_{Q,c}+\\lambda I)^{-1}X_{Q,c}^{\\top}(t-\\bar t_{\\rm row}),\\qquad\n \\hat s^{\\rm lin}_b=\\clip\\!\\left[\\bar t_{\\rm row}+\\gamma(y_Q-\\bar x_Q)^\\top\\beta_b\\right].\n\\end{equation}\nThe ridge fit itself is row-unweighted; group balancing is used for selecting configurations, not silently substituted into this regression. Per-budget $\\lambda\\in\\{0.1,1,10\\}$ and $\\gamma\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$ minimize source-held-out group-weighted squared error.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L94-L100]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L200-L270]]\n\nThe nonlinear branch retains source response profiles while smoothing them with a Rasch model. Source abilities are fixed at $\\theta_i=\\operatorname{logit}[(\\sum_jX_{ij}+0.5)/(N+1)]$. Item difficulties $d_j$ are fitted by group-weighted Bernoulli likelihood with a quadratic penalty. Write $r_{ij}=\\sigmoid(\\theta_i-d_j)$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n p_{ij}=\\operatorname{clip}_{[\\epsilon,1-\\epsilon]}\n       [(1-\\rho)r_{ij}+\\rho X_{ij}],\\qquad\n p_{i\\ell j}=\\sigmoid[\\operatorname{logit}(p_{ij})+a_\\ell].\n\\end{equation}\nThe offsets are $a_\\ell=h(-2,-1,0,1,2)_\\ell$ with prior proportions $(1,4,6,4,1)/16$. Each source group's total prior mass is equal; within a group it is divided equally across source rows. Thus this is a mixture of source-derived response profiles and ability offsets, not a new language-model architecture.\n\n--- BENCHMARK PARAGRAPH ---\nThe final assessment contains four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (BBH, GPQA, MuSR, and OpenLLM Math) and six HELM-derived cases (Commonsense, GSM, LegalBench, MATH, MedQA, and MMLU). We retain these staged case names rather than asserting identity with every similarly named public benchmark. Each case uses a fixed binary item universe. The question is prediction for held-out models on that universe, not generalization to newly sampled questions or unseen benchmark distributions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/final_data_manifest.json]]\n\nSeeds 5003, 7001, and 9001 define model-group splits. The evaluator orders groups by a seed-dependent hash, assigns approximately 70\\% of groups to the source side, and withholds the rest as targets. Group construction uses available provenance and conservative family/provider information. It is a metadata heuristic, not a proof of disjoint pretraining or complete ancestry separation. The estimator sees only opaque source-group labels. Source tuning is internal to a fit; final targets are not used to select its settings. Repeated seeds reuse model populations and are not independent populations.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L685-L708]]\n\n--- PROVENANCE TITLES ---\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Vivek2024\",\n  \"title\": \"Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Polo2024\",\n  \"title\": \"tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Kipnis2025\",\n  \"title\": \"metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Pacchiardi2024\",\n  \"title\": \"100 instances is all you need: predicting the success of a new LLM on unseen data by testing on a few instances\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Li2025\",\n  \"title\": \"Active Evaluation Acquisition for Efficient LLM Benchmarking\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Liang2023\",\n  \"title\": \"Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Wang2024\",\n  \"title\": \"MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Hoerl1970\",\n  \"title\": \"Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Elfving1952\",\n  \"title\": \"Optimum Allocation in Linear Regression Theory\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Kiefer1960\",\n  \"title\": \"The Equivalence of Two Extremum Problems\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Andersen1970\",\n  \"title\": \"Asymptotic Properties of Conditional Maximum-Likelihood Estimators\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Dempster1977\",\n  \"title\": \"Maximum Likelihood from Incomplete Data Via the EM Algorithm\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Hastie2009\",\n  \"title\": \"The Elements of Statistical Learning\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Bishop2006\",\n  \"title\": \"Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Efron1979\",\n  \"title\": \"Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Rubin1976\",\n  \"title\": \"Inference and Missing Data\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Little2019\",\n  \"title\": \"Statistical Analysis with Missing Data\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Roberts2017\",\n  \"title\": \"Cross‐validation strategies for data with temporal, spatial, hierarchical, or phylogenetic structure\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Varma2006\",\n  \"title\": \"Bias in error estimation when using cross-validation for model selection\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Arlot2010\",\n  \"title\": \"A survey of cross-validation procedures for model selection\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Stone1974\",\n  \"title\": \"Cross-Validatory Choice and Assessment of Statistical Predictions\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Kendall1938\",\n  \"title\": \"A NEW MEASURE OF RANK CORRELATION\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Spearman1904\",\n  \"title\": \"The Proof and Measurement of Association between Two Things\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"BenDavid2010\",\n  \"title\": \"A theory of learning from different domains\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n{\n  \"citation_key\": \"Brier1950\",\n  \"title\": \"VERIFICATION OF FORECASTS EXPRESSED IN TERMS OF PROBABILITY\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n\n--- ANDERSEN PROVENANCE ---\n{\n  \"id\": \"ref.openalex.W2117450242\",\n  \"paper_id\": \"https://openalex.org/W2117450242\",\n  \"citation_key\": \"Andersen1970\",\n  \"title\": \"Asymptotic Properties of Conditional Maximum-Likelihood Estimators\",\n  \"year\": 1970,\n  \"venue\": \"Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B (Statistical Methodology)\",\n  \"query\": \"Exact DOI: 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1970.tb00842.x\",\n  \"source_url\": \"https://api.openalex.org/works/https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1970.tb00842.x\",\n  \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-13T18:27:35.978252+00:00\",\n  \"abstract\": \"Summary The problem of obtaining consistent estimates for structural parameters in the presence of infinitely many incidental parameters was discussed first by Neyman and Scott (1948). In this paper a maximum-likelihood method based on the conditional distribution given minimal sufficient statistics for the incidental parameters is suggested. It is proved that conditional maximum-likelihood estimates in the regular case are consistent and asymptotically normally distributed with a simple asymptotic variance. The efficiency problem of this new estimator is discussed in particular with respect to some situations with ancillary information.\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1111/j.2517-6161.1970.tb00842.x\",\n  \"consulted_scope\": \"OpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\"\n}\n\n--- MEMORY FOOTER ---\n- evidence: build_paper_artifacts.py derives 28/30 common case-seed records from final.evidence.episodes. Conditional primary error is lower than fitted uniform in nine fixed cases, except MedQA, but Commonsense has one supported seed and qualification remains failed. Replaced unrelated bibliography with 25 exact-DOI vetted sources using OpenAlex after S2 429. Rejected whole-aggregate selection-inferiority interpretation and isolated profile-benefit claims. One uniform-subset description was corrected after evaluator inspection showed identical seeded uniform orders.\n- next_action: Finalize claim map and reproducibility documentation, verify artifacts, inspect body occupancy, and retain external validator portability limitations without claiming unavailable checks passed.\n\n### 2026-11-13T18:50:33.354571+00:00 | step=verify | scope=paper\n- summary: Grounding and rendered-paper checks passed locally; canonical wrapper portability limitations retained; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Validation does not establish new method recommendations.\n- evidence: 30 grounded claims, 25 inspected exact-DOI references, 903 condition/case/seed/budget metric rows with missing predictions retained as missing. Zero dangling evidence links. Eight rendered body pages with geometric last-page fill about 0.896; substantive SI starts on page 11. Main scientific result remains completed/not_qualified. Canonical figure script lacks src.orchestrator; canonical recorder rejects a standard symlink crossing approved TeX roots. Local schema, resolved-recorder-root, reaggregation, pointer, language, bibliography and rendered-SI checks passed. Corrected a numerical correlation guard to match evaluator peak-to-peak constant-vector handling rather than floating-point std. No estimator experiments or final reruns.\n- next_action: Finalize provenance and memory; hand off overview placeholder for synchronous orchestrator rendering and final parent validation.\n\n### 2026-11-13T18:50:52.766140+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with inspected grounded inputs; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Final handoff adds no empirical recommendation.\n- evidence: Compiled anonymous ICLR manuscript, 30-claim mapping, 25-reference bibliography/provenance, quantitative vector/raster figures, exhaustive descriptive metrics ledger, readable evaluated-source snapshots and reproducibility instructions are present. All observed qualification failures, missing records, structural-check limitations, fallback uncertainty and unknown underlying causes remain explicit. No search-performance or component-causality claims were substituted. Scientific-evidence verifier has zero dangling links. Overview remains the required guarded placeholder for the orchestrator-owned render; parent validation limitations are explicitly recorded rather than claimed passed.\n- next_action: Stop manuscript writing; orchestrator should render the overview synchronously, then compile and run its canonical full validation.\n\n### 2026-11-13T18:54:26.803424+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: Draft-gap analysis ready with two medium-priority, paragraph-specific retrieval targets; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Citation triage adds no empirical method recommendation.\n- evidence: Reviewed manuscript, 25 existing references and provenance, 30 grounded claims, final-evaluation context, strategy, and qualitative global summary. Existing named LLM comparator families are directly cited. Outputs draft_reference_gaps.json and draft_reference_gaps.md request only Leaderboard collection context and direct Rasch/2PL foundations. JSON structure, existing section names, exact draft anchors, and memo agreement passed. SHA-256 checks confirm paper.tex, references.bib, and references_provenance.json are unchanged. No reference retrieval or experiment was run. The apply_patch utility was unavailable; the two new report files were created with shell heredocs.\n- next_action: Stop reason: draft_reference_gaps_ready. A subsequent authorized retrieval stage may seek approximately two to three sources across these targets, reuse existing HELM support, and preserve staged benchmark identities and qualification limits.\n\n\n--- CONCLUSIONS LOCATION ---\n_idea_generation\nattempts_history.jsonl\nbest_program_manifest.json\nconclusions.md\nconclusions_history.jsonl\nconclusions_other_workers.md\nevents.jsonl\nidea_generation_failures.jsonl\nideas\nideas_history.jsonl\nlatest_run_manifest.json\nmemory.jsonl\nmemory.md\nouter_state.json\ntask\n\n--- MEMORY HELPER OPTIONS ---\n6:* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n222:    parser.add_argument(\n228:    parser.add_argument(\"--event-json\", default=None, help=\"Event payload as JSON object string\")\n229:    parser.add_argument(\"--event-file\", default=None, help=\"Path to event JSON file\")\n230:    parser.add_argument(\n231:        \"--local-memory-md\",\n232:        default=\"./memory.md\",\n235:    parser.add_argument(\n236:        \"--global-memory-md\",\n240:    parser.add_argument(\n246:    parser.add_argument(\n251:    parser.add_argument(\n256:    parser.add_argument(\n261:    parser.add_argument(\n267:    parser.add_argument(\n273:    parser.add_argument(\n278:    parser.add_argument(\n388:    if not args.global_memory_md or not args.global_memory_jsonl:\n390:            \"append_memory: --global-memory-md and --global-memory-jsonl are required \"\n400:    local_memory_path = Path(args.local_memory_md)\n401:    global_memory_md_path = Path(args.global_memory_md)\n405:    _append_text(global_memory_md_path, _markdown_entry(event), header=GLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
