{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- EDIT TOOL ---\\\\n'; command -v apply_patch || true; printf '\\\\n--- PROVENANCE SUMMARY ---\\\\n'; jq -r '.count, .retrieval_note, (.references[] | [.title, .consulted_scope] | @tsv)' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- ANDERSEN ABSTRACT ---\\\\n'; jq -r '.references[] | select(.title | startswith(\\\"Asymptotic\\\")) | .abstract' references_provenance.json; printf '\\\\n--- CONCLUSIONS ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory/conclusions.md; printf '\\\\n--- INPUT HASHES ---\\\\n'; sha256sum paper.tex references.bib references_provenance.json draft_reference_gaps.md draft_reference_gaps.json global_memory_summary.md reference_query_plan.json\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- EDIT TOOL ---\n\n--- PROVENANCE SUMMARY ---\n25\nSemantic Scholar exact-ID requests returned HTTP 429. OpenAlex exact-DOI fallback. Replaces unrelated supplied retrieval hits; no broad search or citation traversal.\nAnchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\ntinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nmetabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\n100 instances is all you need: predicting the success of a new LLM on unseen data by testing on a few instances\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nActive Evaluation Acquisition for Efficient LLM Benchmarking\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nHolistic Evaluation of Language Models\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nMMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nRidge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nOptimum Allocation in Linear Regression Theory\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nThe Equivalence of Two Extremum Problems\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nAsymptotic Properties of Conditional Maximum-Likelihood Estimators\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nMaximum Likelihood from Incomplete Data Via the EM Algorithm\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nThe Elements of Statistical Learning\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nPattern Recognition and Machine Learning\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nBootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nInference and Missing Data\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nStatistical Analysis with Missing Data\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nCross‐validation strategies for data with temporal, spatial, hierarchical, or phylogenetic structure\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nBias in error estimation when using cross-validation for model selection\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nA survey of cross-validation procedures for model selection\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nCross-Validatory Choice and Assessment of Statistical Predictions\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nA NEW MEASURE OF RANK CORRELATION\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nThe Proof and Measurement of Association between Two Things\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nA theory of learning from different domains\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\nVERIFICATION OF FORECASTS EXPRESSED IN TERMS OF PROBABILITY\tOpenAlex title, metadata and abstract where available; no citation links traversed\n\n--- ANDERSEN ABSTRACT ---\nSummary The problem of obtaining consistent estimates for structural parameters in the presence of infinitely many incidental parameters was discussed first by Neyman and Scott (1948). In this paper a maximum-likelihood method based on the conditional distribution given minimal sufficient statistics for the incidental parameters is suggested. It is proved that conditional maximum-likelihood estimates in the regular case are consistent and asymptotically normally distributed with a simple asymptotic variance. The efficiency problem of this new estimator is discussed in particular with respect to some situations with ancillary information.\n\n--- CONCLUSIONS ---\nScope grouping claims to the interface: use supplied opaque source groups for leakage-controlled validation; reserve identity/explicit-ancestry stress tests for authorized offline metadata, and never infer lineage from architecture or invent ancestry. Retain source-only benchmark-mean conditional-covariance acquisition as a lead, with same-predictor D-optimal and strong IRT controls. Explore a group-balanced mixture of Rasch-shrunk source response profiles with ability offsets as an untested nonlinear estimator on identical fixed prefixes; use grouped tuning with a zero-weight linear fallback to isolate estimator complementarity. Treat offsets as global-strength extrapolation, not protection against unseen skills, and do not interpret posterior concentration as calibrated target uncertainty. Test calibration and group weighting separately, refitting acquisition and all profile/prior fitting inside grouped source folds. Historical aggregate method differences do not establish paired causal effects or explain failed acceptance; source-tuned gains and post-selection uncertainty are not independent target evidence. If revisiting a uniform-complement residual audit, reserve conditional design-unbiasedness for its frozen-predictor, full-correction, unclipped endpoint, not shrinkage or clipped variants. Compare paired equal-budget methods with group-level uncertainty before claiming improvement; no accepted improvement has been established. Respect absent target full scores and target group identities, keep diagnostics internal, and report inaccessible experiments as incomplete without weakening scientific or paper requirements. Unavailable incumbent source and correct=false alone are not evidence of an implementation defect.\n\n--- INPUT HASHES ---\n6c6b38f111751a92dab4b9b89b773420d5ee21aeee1e9eee69ac9d32d9182adc  paper.tex\nf8155117c64188f68b2b3c101dcaf615c10d6acdebab15a1484165f19c18b22f  references.bib\ne52c313a6020bf0865edfb424e47558620ca83116896d55547ae9550092a9b25  references_provenance.json\n54b9053c4823f87eb7c68fa9cc4dc26e71df52d4a6ee08807a006611eb166b9c  draft_reference_gaps.md\n7f3186f3ba46c8b9f28135171b9e569fc45453cc1fcb48f5fab7f44494afc29e  draft_reference_gaps.json\n72b16e54c8e961573302694857761c3d4b38696d1ea722e8a96af9f4ef9a2c69  global_memory_summary.md\nf699fc51e9e94de26d351fdfbf1a255c906dfc00431850523deb1efe54d7c03b  reference_query_plan.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
